# Conditional native-root mechanism and exact proof limits

## 概述

Direct isolated Python cannot prove family absence during interpreter/native/import startup. A new native root could establish the collector before Python begins and retain it through cleanup. This is a formal design proposal, not a minimal patch or implemented owner proof. Old original-worker AST claims cannot be reused to establish this native program.

## 对应需求

FR-1 binds actual root and pre-Python native launch barrier. FR-2 specifies no-launch versus launch-possible family and IO evidence. FR-3 separates abort closure from command success and demands finite review.

## 技术方案

1. Parent constructs an immutable reviewed native executable image. One candidate mechanism is a memfd written with bounded static ELF bytes, sealed against write/grow/shrink/seal changes before any exec, then consumed by actual descriptor-based exec. This is not yet implemented; target-kernel execution semantics, flags, ownership and source/build identity require actual review and controls. A plain path plus pre/post hash does not bind the executing image. Static ELF must have no uncontrolled PT_INTERP/DT_NEEDED or initializers; source and real native build mapping must be audited. No success is inferred from a future binary merely being called static.
2. Parent actually creates one private native root (known Popen/pidfd, private protocol stdin/stdout, stderr DEVNULL honestly unobservable). Root has no unrelated children and no Python runtime. Reviewed single-thread bootstrap verifies PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER before any fork and validates its own native empty child table, then emits BOOTREADY. Native source has no child-creation path before receiving complete valid LAUNCH. Initial ECHILD belongs to this exact root's child table, not the application's global table.
3. Parent marks `launch_write_attempted` before its first native write syscall, even if canceled/partial/zero later. Only an actual owner lifecycle with no attempt may take the no-launch branch; external NoG events/JSON/booleans cannot prove it. No-launch root failure can only produce an aborted-startup-close outcome after real same created pidfd terminal/IO retirement, never command success.
4. After LAUNCH, root actually creates a Python child (prefer a kernel creation primitive returning its pidfd, with reviewed fallback actual ownership verification). Source/pidfd and private pipes are bound to that exact creation. Original worker body could remain byte-exact if it is actually executed, but its parent role and startup argv changed. READY pid/ppid/pgid validators must be formally updated for root versus Python child; old Popen-child validator semantics cannot simply be reused. Original worker may not inherit the native protocol writer or privileged source descriptors (CLOEXEC/explicit close), so its JSON cannot forge native EMPTY.
5. STOP operates within the original single 2s cleanup deadline. Root stays alive and acts as the genuine private subreaper while Python startup/import/command descendants exist. Its exact created-child pidfd and later actual adopted-child waitid(P_PIDFD) authority are required. Proc/PID/PPid are bounded discovery candidates only; each adopted candidate requires real kernel own-child verification before pidfd signal/reap. A child entering another process group/session does not leave actual ancestry/adoption; killpg alone is never family proof. Roots or descendants that cannot be reaped/retired before the deadline retain UNKNOWN. No blanket SIGTERM exit is a grant.
6. Because the native root itself creates only this family and remains alive as the installed subreaper, its own actual P_ALL ECHILD after all reaps establishes an empty attributable family only under the declared ancestry/kernel trust boundary. Parent application's ECHILD is unrelated. No new fork can occur after genuine EMPTY because no family child remains and root source has transitioned to a no-launch terminal state. Every root-owned family transport pipe must be drained to real EOF within the limit and physically retired before EMPTY. Child-private FD closure is only evidenced by that actual child kernel terminal/reap, not by invented explicit-close receipts for inaccessible descriptors. Unexpected output, missing EOF or unknown close refuses EMPTY. Original stderr DEVNULL has no EOF and is not listed as such; any new child stderr PIPE would be a declared IO-semantic change requiring controls.
7. Parent receives a typed new source-root EMPTY event over its actual private pipe, sends final native close request, and requires same root terminal, own stdout EOF/buffer empty and actual stdin/stdout/pidfd closes. This event is a local lifecycle statement, never a publisher/service issuer grant. If root dies before collectible family emptiness, parent retains native family uncertainty; root terminal alone is insufficient after LAUNCH.

## 文件结构

Only these three documents are delivered. Proposed new native root source, executable-source loader, ProcessOwner phase integration and meaningful ordinary controls are absent and UNKNOWN impact. Original process source and original worker bytes remain unchanged. Any implementation creates a new source tree/build artifact/runtime identity and must not be relabeled pure3084.

## Threat, phase and FD boundaries

Trust requires actual Linux kernel pidfd/waitid/subreaper semantics, an immutable reviewed executing image, no foreign privileged ptrace/process injection and private protocol descriptors. A collector is not generic machine supervision: inaccessible or escaping attribution, native-source uncertainty, inherited protocol writers, launch partial-write, actor cancellation and effects UNKNOWN all retain denial. Phase mutation belongs to the same product owner immediately before syscalls; persisted observations cannot authorize it. Exact ownership ledger is recorded before metadata checks; every actual close is attempted once independently without masking original primary or cancellation objects.

## Concrete feasibility decision

This architecture can remove the specific pre-subreaper Python startup gap if and only if the new native source/build/immutable-exec/collector/IO protocol is actually implemented and independently established. No such artifacts exist now. Therefore production implementation eligibility remains OPEN pending formal scope review, and no closed/command/pass/install/release claim is made. It does not ask for impossible coverage of all Python asynchronous bytecode windows; it moves family attribution before Python and defines actual partial-write uncertainty. Relative to a direct Python signal patch this is substantial new native machinery, not evidence that the old original method will pass.

## Original deadlines and command roles

The original .2 READY deadline still includes the entire new root bootstrap plus Python startup through original worker READY; BOOTREADY does not restart or replace it. A LAUNCH attempt can precede worker READY while no request/G has been dispatched, so that timeout takes the launch-possible collector branch rather than the no-launch sole-child branch. No probe duration is claimed to solve .2 cold startup. Root collector must not share the Python child kill target: parent must not retain the old unconditional killpg against a group containing the only collector. Exact child pidfds/own-kernel adoption determine stop targets, and root death before EMPTY preserves uncertainty. Original worker command DONE/status/reaped constraints remain mandatory for command results; native EMPTY only supplies a separately validated lifecycle closure and cannot fabricate a DONE frame.

## Mandatory native birth-path proof — independent review V2

The transition from caller into the created root is part of the trusted source boundary, not an invisible Popen implementation detail. Python `preexec_fn`, executing a Python loader in the child before fexecve, and user atfork callbacks are prohibited: they would restore the uncontrolled pre-collector fork window. A parent PID plus a later memfd hash cannot bind that transition.

There are two conditional mechanism choices, neither implemented or proven here. A native posix_spawn path can be considered only if its exact CPython/libc/native spawn implementation and file-actions path are bound and independently shown to execute no user Python/atfork callbacks in the created child. It must actually execute the sealed source descriptor (with reviewed descriptor inheritance/CLOEXEC semantics), report real exec success, and bind its returned actual child to an owned pidfd/own-child kernel check; a mutable `/proc/...` path string by itself is not the executable-binding proof. Unknown spawn fallback/exec status refuses the no-launch proof.

A stronger alternative is a reviewed native birth primitive: actual clone3(CLONE_PIDFD) returning the created root pidfd, with a native-only child trampoline that establishes/checks subreaper and executes the sealed image via execveat, using only explicitly audited syscalls before exec. No Python return/callback/atfork code may run in that child branch. This requires a new native module/binary and source-build/runtime binding for the trampoline itself, plus true failure/exec-status handling and private FD retirement. It cannot be represented by constructing a Popen or owner from an external numeric PID. Exact target-kernel support and trusted source mapping are mandatory; if unavailable, no fake adapter or silent fallback.

Both choices must audit the actual native pre-main CRT/init path as well as main: static/no-PT_INTERP is insufficient to prove no child creation. Birth failures and pre-BOOTREADY root death qualify as sole-root abort only if this entire actual executed birth/initialization path is source-bound and proven to have no descendant creation. Otherwise native family state remains UNKNOWN even with a terminal pidfd and no LAUNCH attempt. Current implementation eligibility remains OPEN until one birth mechanism and the collector/IO/source proof are independently approved; no currently available library call is claimed to satisfy it.
